SM(511,{"n":"Romans 6","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 6.mp3","d":2361.5,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.16,"We'll go ahead and get started."],[24.0,"And uh why don't we get started in just a moment?"],[26.16,"We're gonna be picking up at the beginning of St."],[28.8,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans, chapter six."],[32.24,"Uh it's been a little while since we did the second half of chapter five, and the second half of chapter five was pretty dense as we worked through it."],[42.48,"We talked about how the second half of Romans chapter five, beginning with verse twelve, is probably one of the most, I guess you could say, controversial passages in the Bible."],[56.48,"Uh, in that it's the text from which the doctrine of original sin developed in the West."],[63.56,"And so that's one of the major differences between Eastern Christianity and Western Christianity."],[69.48,"It's also one of the main texts used in the Reformation to argue for a lot of Protestant doctrines."],[77.16,"But I'm not going to go through all that again because it took us a fair amount of time last time."],[82.28,"So eventually, I can't really say soon anymore, that Bible study will be available on ancient faith."],[89.72,"Uh, by the time anyone's hearing this recording, it'll already be available."],[93.56,"Um, but our lead time right now is close to a year."],[97.64,"So it's gonna be a while."],[100.28,"But to get us caught up, as we mentioned all the way back at the beginning, and again, by the time anyone's hearing this, the first Bible study on St."],[104.52,"and Paul's Epistle of the Romans will also be available, and you can go back listen to the introduction."],[113.36,"But in Rome, uh under the Emperor Claudius, uh, the Jewish community had been expelled from the city for a period of a couple of years, and so this had sort of fragmented the Christian community in the city of Rome, because of course the Christian community, there were Jewish Christians and Gentile Christians at the church in Rome."],[134.88,"A big chunk of them, the Jewish Christians had been uh expelled from the city, and the Gentile Christians had sort of gone on on their own, and now, after this period of time where they've gotten used to going on on their own, the Jewish Christians have now returned."],[149.84,"And so St"],[152.12,"Paul is concerned about making sure that the church sort of reintegrates itself, that we don't end up having two churches, this one Jewish Christian church and this other Gentile Christian church for everybody else."],[162.84,"It's two separate things."],[164.84,"And so, as we've been going through uh St."],[167.88,"Paul's epistle so far, he's been trying to show how all human beings face the same, face the same problems, have to deal with the same enemies, and how Christ is the solution to sort of the peril in which human beings as such find themselves."],[186.52,"And so, in earlier chapters, he started by going back to Abraham, because of course, the main thing which the the Jewish people saw as separating them from Gentile people."],[199.04,"Well, one of them."],[200.16,"The two main things were one being descended from Abraham and two having received the Torah, having received God's revelation in the Old Testament, and having the law and the customs that derive from it."],[213.52,"So St. Paul started by going back before the law to Abraham, because of course Abraham lived a good at least 500 years before Moses received the Torah, and saying, Well, let's look at how Abraham became God's friend."],[229.86,"Let's look at uh how Abraham encountered God."],[232.74,"And this was all before the law."],[234.18,"It was before even Abraham was circumcised."],[236.74,"So the law wasn't in the picture then."],[238.34,"The law got Torah got. added later"],[241.9,"And then in uh Romans chapter five that we just uh discussed, he went back even further than that."],[248.94,"He went all the way back to Adam."],[250.76,"He said, so this includes now everybody, because everybody's descended from Adam."],[254.36,"And talked about how our real problems are not whether we're keeping the Torah or not."],[260.12,"It's not whether we have tassels on the fringes of our garments."],[263.8,"It's not whether we eat pork."],[265.56,"The real problem we have is that everyone sins and therefore everyone dies."],[271.97,"Going back to Adam."],[272.85,"And it doesn't matter if you're a Jew or a Gentile, you sin and you die."],[278.45,"And so Christ is the solution to that for everybody, regardless of. ethnicity, culture"],[286.25,"And so this is sort of where we ended last time was St."],[291.61,"Paul making that point that through the sin that began with Adam, death came into the world."],[300.73,"Death reigned over the world until Christ came."],[304.73,"And now, through Christ's death and resurrection, now we as human beings receive life, eternal life, and that life is spreading now with the gospel to everyone."],[317.45,"And so now he's been talking about this in a in a broad sense."],[322.91,"Talking about Adam and Christ, the first man, the second Adam."],[327.71,"Making this big theological point"],[330.81,"About life and death and Christ and Adam and Sin, power of sin."],[335.37,"And now in chapter six, as we start here, he's going to narrow this down to okay, here's how this gets applied to you."],[344.19,"As a person who's part of the Christian community in Rome."],[350.03,"Here's how this manifests itself in your experience, in your actual life."],[356.11,"So unless we have any leftover questions or comments or mutterings or grumblings or anything else, we'll go ahead and get started in St."],[367.79,"Paul's Epistle of the Romans, chapter 6, verse 1."],[370.99,"What shall we say then?"],[372.03,"Shall we continue to sin that grace may abound"],[377.03,"Certainly not. How shall we who died to sin live any longer in it?"],[382.95,"So he's picking up where he left off."],[386.31,"Right at the end of chapter 5, he talked about how the law, the Torah, had been added, had been given later, so that trespasses would increase."],[396.55,"And what he meant by that was he had made a distinction earlier in chapter 5 where he said, Well, look, Adam, Adam sinned by God gave him a commandment."],[406.79,"God directly told him."],[408.25,"You can eat from all the trees, don't eat from this tree."],[410.73,"Adam went and did it anyway."],[413.13,"So he sort of deliberately sinned."],[415.29,"He knew the rule."],[416.61,"He chose to break the rule."],[418.61,"He died. Okay."],[420.61,"But St. Paul made the point that even people later."],[425.42,"We're now thousands of years later."],[427.6,"Minimally. Even people who God never directly gave a command, like all those Gentiles who didn't receive the Torah."],[434.88,"God didn't give them commandments directly."],[437.76,"They still sinned and they still died."],[440.4,"They just didn't know what sin was exactly."],[445.65,"And what to do about it."],[447.57,"So he's made the point that the Torah is added so that now people will know what sin is."],[452.85,"Sins become trespasses."],[454.33,"Because now you know the rules that you're breaking."],[457.41,"You're not just sort of wandering, trying to figure out what to do."],[460.11,"You now know what the sins are, and you have a means of dealing with them"],[465.99,"Right, because the Torah included the whole sacrificial system for repentance."],[470.07,"So this gives you a huge advantage knowing, being able to see what your sins are, knowing how to repent of them."],[478.09,"And be reconciled to God."],[480.01,"Okay, so the Torah gets added so that trespasses will increase."],[484.73,"People will know that they're sinning."],[488.01,"And because they know, now they can repent."],[490.01,"And so he says, Where sin abounded."],[493.13,"Now there's all these trespasses, but God's grace also abounded."],[497.46,"Because now there was repentance and there was forgiveness of those sins."],[502.1,"So he starts chapter six by saying, Well, so then should we just go ahead and just keep sinning and not worry about it."],[508.83,"Because, hey, you know, we may the more we sin, the more God forgives."],[514.48,"So God's forgiveness comes in."],[517.44,"Should we just not worry about it?"],[519.26,"Of course, he says, no."],[522.35,"He actually says more than no."],[523.95,"What's translated as certainly not, this is the New King James, is actually a really odd form of a Greek verb."],[532.27,"It's only used 64 times in the whole New Testament."],[536.03,"And uh 48 of them are St."],[540.35,"Paul saying what's translated as certainly not here."],[544.43,"It's called an optative verb, but it's a really old, antiquated form of verb."],[551.11,"But he's using this weird form."],[557.03,"This is a super strong no."],[558.79,"This is heck no."],[560.4,"This is that's why they say certainly not, you know, which is sort of the more polite version."],[567.92,"That idea is obviously clueless."],[570.1,"This is and then he he further he begins his next point by saying, How shall we who died to sin live any longer in it?"],[581.54,"Now, in terms of what he means by that, he's gonna go on and explain."],[586.1,"So in verse 2, or do you not know that as many of us as we' were' breap btiapzedti intozed into Christ Jesus, his death."],[594.62,"Okay. Now notice, you know, we're used to seeing Jesus Christ and now we see Christ Jesus."],[601.8,"The word order is reversed."],[603.96,"Don't read a ton into that."],[606.38,"Because in Greek, word order doesn't matter."],[609.02,"In English, word order is very important."],[611.6,"We do our sentences subject, verb, object."],[614.32,"They don't do that in Greek."],[615.52,"In Greek, the form of the word tells you what part of speech it is."],[620.16,"So you can put them in any order you want."],[622.56,"Which really helps if you're writing poetry and stuff."],[626.01,"Or writing hymns, because you can just move everything around, so it sounds good."],[630.25,"But what it should remind us is once again. and I make this point only because every once in a while somebody asks me a question"],[638.93,"Christ is not Jesus' last name."],[643.03,"I actually sold it."],[647.19,"Yeah. So, yeah, it's not his last name."],[651.43,"It's not St."],[652.47,"Joseph and St."],[653.35,"Mary's last name."],[655.51,"That they that they passed on to him."],[658.15,"Um I once had somebody ask me what Jesus' middle name was because they thought that was his last name."],[665.59,"But it's a title."],[667.37,"Remember, it's it's Christos in Greek is is Mashiach, in Hebrew Messiah."],[673.33,"right Which means the anointed king."],[675.07,"So you can say Jesus the king, or you could say King Jesus."],[677.79,"Okay, so that's the equivalent here"],[678.63,"Means the same thing."],[681.51,"Putting it in front is like saying King Jesus."],[686.79,"So this, or do you not know?"],[688.71,"That's his way of saying, here's what I mean."],[692.44,"So he says that that as many of you, we talked about as many of you as have been baptized into Christ, that we sing."],[700.36,"That means everybody."],[702.84,"Every one of you, if you were baptized into Christ."],[707.85,"That means you were baptized into his death."],[712.33,"What does that mean?"],[713.53,"That means when you went under the water, you died with him."],[720.06,"That's what he's saying."],[720.94,"That's why we put people all the way under"],[725.8,"Because getting sprinkled doesn't represent dying."],[730.12,"Nobody ever died from having some water flicked in their face."],[734.76,"Dogs and cats act like they're going to die if you flick water in their face, but no human has ever died from having water flicked in their face."],[751.8,"Therefore, we were buried with him through baptism into death, that just as Christ was raised from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life."],[766.0,"So when you're put under that water, you died."],[770.4,"When you came back up, that was the beginning of a new life."],[774.8,"In the same way that Christ died, and then he rose from the dead."],[781.2,"So what's his point?"],[782.4,"So all the things that characterized you before you were baptized, back before you were a Christian."],[788.48,"Before you were baptized, now are dead."],[792.16,"What's he referring back to?"],[793.52,"Well, he's referring back to Adam."],[796.0,"Remember, he says sin came into the world through one man."],[800.26,"Through Adam, and then through sin, death came to everyone."],[806.66,"Because all sinned."],[808.5,"So we've all followed his pattern."],[810.1,"All the way down to us"],[811.42,"Like father, like son, and daughter."],[815.64,"We've sinned like him, and so we've died like him."],[818.44,"And when we're born, we're born with the fallen human nature that we inherit from him."],[827.89,"All the way down."],[828.53,"So we're born mortal."],[829.89,"We're not born immortal, and then if we sin at some point, all of a sudden, well, now we can die."],[835.7,"We're born mortal."],[837.06,"We inherit, we inherit that."],[840.66,"Paul is saying is that person, that person who you were, with everything you inherited, died when you went into the water."],[850.42,"Dead. And the person you became when you came out of the water was a new person, a different person, who's now after the pattern of Christ in his resurrection."],[863.58,"So this is related to the concept of eternal life."],[867.5,"We talked about that this a little bit when we were reading St."],[869.9,"John's gospel. We tend to think of eternal life as something that comes after this."],[875.98,"We die, you know, we our soul goes somewhere."],[880.4,"Now the resurrection happens, we get raised from the dead, and then eternal life starts."],[884.96,"That's where it starts when we get when we get resurrected when Christ returns."],[889.52,"What St We start a new life then."],[891.42,"That's not what St."],[892.46,"Paul is saying here."],[894.38,"Paul is saying here is that when you came up out of the water, that's when your new life started."],[900.38,"That's when your eternal life started."],[902.7,"So eternal life is something that begins now and continues into eternity."],[911.26,"This is important because what's St."],[914.46,"Paul's whole point here?"],[915.66,"St. Paul's whole point here is we can't go on sinning."],[919.42,"We can't go on living the way we used to."],[923.39,"Do you think when when you're when you're raised from the dead on the last day when Christ returns and you're in the kingdom."],[929.43,"Are you going to be sinning?"],[932.83,"This is not a trick question."],[936.12,"Nobody wants to get recorded."],[938.84,"The answer is no, you're not going to be sinning?"],[938.88,"The answer is no."],[940.6,"Okay In the kingdom."],[945.62,"But the point is that kingdom has started now."],[949.22,"You're a member of it now."],[951.62,"And so St."],[952.34,"Paul's asking. So how could you go on sinning now?"],[956.34,"How could you go on living the way you used to?"],[960.66,"Back before. It's not possible."],[965.06,"It's not possible."],[968.9,"And he's making this argument for a couple of reasons."],[972.82,"The main one, though, being what's the criticism that's being leveled against him by some of the Jewish community?"],[981.3,"Well, he's teaching against the law."],[984.18,"He's saying you shouldn't follow the Torah."],[986.46,"He's saying we could just get rid of it."],[988.56,"He's saying it's just this extra thing."],[990.74,"He's saying you don't have to keep the commandments anymore."],[994.04,"You could just you could just live however."],[996.77,"And so St."],[997.49,"Paul's making the point."],[998.69,"No, no, no, no, no, quite the opposite."],[1001.27,"Quite the opposite."],[1002.31,"It's impossible to continue living the way you used to."],[1007.64,"It's impossible. For if we have been united together in the likeness of his death, certainly we also shall be in the likeness of his resurrection."],[1022.12,"Okay. So, see, St."],[1024.76,"Paul uses resurrection and new life both ways."],[1027.4,"He's not denying that there's going to be a resurrection on the last day"],[1033.44,"He's just saying that begins now."],[1035.68,"This is important for understanding how sort of St."],[1038.4,"Paul thinks. There's a bunch of fat fancy words for this, but the simplest way of talking about it is for St."],[1045.84,"Paul, there's an already, and then there's a not yet for all of these things."],[1050.96,"If you're a Christian, you already have all of the promises of Christ in some sense."],[1056.48,"You already have a new life."],[1058.8,"Your eternal life has already started."],[1062.32,"You're already living a different type of life."],[1064.4,"You already have the Holy Spirit."],[1065.76,"There are all these things that are already true."],[1070.0,"But those same things. in their fullness are not yet"],[1078.12,"We don't have them yet."],[1080.76,"We're literally going to be raised from the dead when Christ returns."],[1092.44,"Both of those are true."],[1095.24,"And they're not two separate things for St."],[1099.8,"Paul. For St."],[1101.4,"Paul, the kingdom has already begun, and the kingdom is going to come."],[1108.14,"Both are true."],[1110.62,"At the same time."],[1112.54,"Knowing this, that our old man was crucified with him. that the body of sin might be done away with, that we should no longer be slaves of sin"],[1124.26,"For he who has died has been freed from sin."],[1128.18,"Okay. So he says, he reiterates."],[1134.12,"This person who you were, who was a slave of sin, is dead."],[1139.97,"If you would baptize, that person is dead."],[1143.03,"Dead people can't sin anymore."],[1146.2,"Again, not a trick question."],[1147.82,"They're just laying there."],[1148.7,"They can't. They don't have bodies."],[1151.72,"So they're. So they're dead."],[1156.08,"Now he brings in the idea of slavery here. which is which is important"],[1162.28,"I've mentioned this before, but but for Saint Paul, there's a distinction between sin and sins."],[1171.56,"Okay. So we talk about sins, and when we talk about sins plural, we mean like what St."],[1178.2,"Paul was talking about with trespasses."],[1180.46,"God gave a commandment, you broke it."],[1183.07,"That we say that's a sin."],[1185.41,"And then people who when we do that a lot, we have a whole bunch of sins, plural, to confess and to repent of."],[1192.05,"Okay. But St."],[1193.57,"Paul also talks about just sin in general."],[1199.15,"Like in Romans 5 in the last chapter when he, tal.k about sin reigning through death"],[1205.4,"That sin is sort of, or I'm sorry, it was actually death reigning through sin."],[1209.48,"But that that death reigns through sin."],[1211.66,"That death is like this overlord and through the power of sin."],[1215.28,"Where sin is this power."],[1218.24,"And that goes all the way back to almost the very beginning of the Old Testament in Genesis 4, when Cain is angry at his brother Abel and is thinking about killing him."],[1229.36,"God comes to him and says, Cain's sin is crouching at your door."],[1234.0,"He's talking about like it's an animal."],[1235.7,"That's out there ready to pounce on him."],[1238.02,"He says, It seeks to master you, you must master it."],[1242.4,"He says there's gonna be this battle between you and sin."],[1244.72,"And of course, we know Cain doesn't"],[1245.4,"It's gonna try and overcome you."],[1247.16,"You need to overcome it."],[1250.76,"Cain falls prey to it."],[1253.0,"But that's how St."],[1254.12,"Paul is talking about sin here when he talks about being slaves of sin."],[1258.77,"That sin comes in and masters us."],[1261.65,"Sin comes and takes control of us."],[1266.05,"We tend to think of it again as these sort of voluntary actions."],[1270.15,"Of sins. But this is sin as a force or a power."],[1274.95,"This is why in the Orthodox Church we talk about the passions."],[1279.29,"The sinful passions."],[1281.29,"Passion comes from the same root as passive."],[1285.61,"It's something that acts upon us."],[1287.37,"We become passive and it takes control."],[1290.63,"Our pride isn't just we choose to do something that is proud sometimes."],[1296.81,"But pride actually takes control of us and controls our actions."],[1301.3,"I do this instead of that because of my pride."],[1306.66,"Or envy or anger."],[1310.04,"Anger is a pretty obvious example."],[1312.92,"We become angry and we're not we're not in control."],[1315.72,"We're not choosing to do what we do now."],[1317.72,"Our anger is in control, and we do things, and then afterwards we look back and regret them."],[1323.48,"We say we lost control."],[1325.72,"Sloth is another very obvious example."],[1328.14,"Laziness, we're not doing anything."],[1330.54,"Lust. same thing"],[1332.8,"People talk about, you know, they get fired up with passion and they do things that they then regret because it takes control of us."],[1341.04,"Okay. So this is what this is what St."],[1343.28,"Paul is talking about."],[1344.16,"It's not just that, oh yeah, once in a while we did some bad things in our past life."],[1350.08,"It's that sin had taken control of us."],[1354.64,"That's why it's not enough just to go and offer the 87th sacrifice."],[1361.78,"For this week, you know, the 87th goat for this week's sins."],[1365.95,"That's not really dealing with the problem."],[1368.64,"It's managing the problem."],[1370.24,"We talked about how the Torah sort of manages sin."],[1373.2,"It sort of keeps it under control"],[1375.7,"But for it to be dealt with, what has to happen?"],[1380.02,"We as slaves have to die."],[1382.26,"Because once we die, if you're a slave."],[1384.84,"Once you die, you're free."],[1388.84,"That's sort of the only way out."],[1392.02,"If you're a slave."],[1393.86,"And so through baptism, through Christ's death and resurrection, we die with him."],[1399.95,"And so now that slavery is over."],[1403.39,"And we rise to a new life where we're free from sin."],[1408.11,"And that's why he's saying that the sins are a result, the individual actions are a result of being controlled by. sin"],[1418.95,"And so if we're no longer the slave of sin, then there's no reason for us to commit those actions anymore."],[1427.45,"Christ has given us this new life."],[1429.53,"And now that we're alive and we're free, we can live in a different way."],[1435.21,"Now, if we died with Christ, we believe that we shall also live with him."],[1440.17,"Knowing that Christ, having been raised from the dead, dies no more."],[1444.65,"Death no longer has dominion over him."],[1446.81,"Christ died, he rose again, he's not going to die again."],[1449.61,"That's the difference between Christ's resurrection and other people who were raised from the dead in the Bible, like Lazarus."],[1457.05,"Lazarus lived a few more years and then he died again."],[1460.41,"The widow of Nain's son lived a few more years and then died again."],[1466.21,"Christ is never going to die again."],[1468.93,"But what St."],[1469.57,"Paul is saying is what that means for us is that if we've died with Christ and been raised to new life, death has no power over us either."],[1481.57,"This is what we celebrate at Posca."],[1483.73,"I know that's 50 days from now, but this is why we celebrate Christ trampling down death by death."],[1491.01,"Because if he's defeated death, then death has no hold over us either."],[1496.13,"For the death that he died, he died to sin once for all."],[1500.45,"But the life that he lives, he lives to God."],[1505.17,"So Christ died to put away sin."],[1505.29,"So he's making a parallel here between Christ's death and resurrection in ours."],[1515.61,"He didn't die to his sins because he didn't have any."],[1518.41,"But this means for us, the sinful person we used to be, the sins we've committed, that's now dead."],[1525.13,"In Christ's case, he very, very literally, after he rose from the dead, he ascended to God the Father."],[1532.19,"So his life was to God very literally."],[1536.24,"To God the Father."],[1537.36,"But for us, we can now live to God."],[1541.81,"Toward God. In God's presence."],[1546.61,"Because we've been set free."],[1550.49,"Likewise, you also reckon yourselves to be dead indeed to sin, but alive to God in Christ Jesus our Lord."],[1558.74,"So he says, consider yourselves, in your head."],[1562.92,"Wrap your head around this."],[1565.64,"That this is what has happened to you."],[1567.48,"If you've been baptized, this is what has happened to you."],[1571.56,"That slavery to sin, that sinful person, the old person is dead."],[1576.92,"Leave them behind."],[1578.7,"Start new. Start again."],[1581.82,"So when St."],[1582.78,"Paul made that statement, did people really understand him?"],[1587.66,"Did they understand the idea of what he was talking about?"],[1591.18,"Because it's kind of very mixed together, and I don't know. how much they knew at that time"],[1595.62,"Well, these are these are people who had heard they hadn't heard Saint Paul preach."],[1602.9,"Most of his letters were written to people in churches where he'd been."],[1606.66,"A lot of them were churches he started."],[1608.42,"So they had heard him preach for some time."],[1610.74,"So he was they sort of would know what he was talking about."],[1613.94,"Church in Rome he hasn't been to yet."],[1616.58,"So, but they have received preaching from other people."],[1627.82,"Paul is talking about baptism."],[1630.86,"Because the apostles at the time, they had a way they baptized people."],[1635.14,"And so he could refer them back to that."],[1638.18,"This was a liturgical thing."],[1640.06,"They'd been baptized, they'd seen people baptized."],[1643.68,"They've been to those services and heard the hymns that were sung and what was preached and that kind of thing."],[1649.84,"So he's referring them back to that."],[1653.86,"And reminding them of that and explaining more what that means."],[1658.99,"Explaining more what that means."],[1660.51,"So that's why they kind of grasped onto it."],[1664.83,"We're not living in that culture at that time."],[1668.27,"You know what I mean?"],[1669.31,"And so that's why I have to do all this explaining."],[1673.63,"That's why we have to have Bible studies."],[1675.76,"It's like when when Metropolitan Joseph sends a letter to the church, like he just did, it's going to be in the bullet to this week, if you haven't seen it on the internet already."],[1681.84,"We read it and we understand what he's talking about"],[1684.2,"He sends a letter to us."],[1687.54,"Even though he probably uses some phrases and stuff."],[1690.74,"That if I took those and I showed it to, you know, somebody, even an English speaker."],[1696.58,"Well, even England."],[1697.54,"Like if I took it to England, there'd probably be some phrases in there that they would like, what's he mean by that?"],[1702.58,"Especially if they weren't Orthodox."],[1704.42,"You know what I mean?"],[1705.06,"There'd be phrases where they'd be like, huh?"],[1707.94,"You know, what does that mean?"],[1709.46,"Or they misinterpret it."],[1714.1,"Because it's being written at our time, and it's being written in the context of the church."],[1718.75,"He could make references to hymns and things that we sing and we recognize them immediately."],[1724.75,"That someone else wouldn't, you know."],[1726.95,"It's the same kind of thing."],[1728.15,"We're just now 2,000 years later, you know."],[1731.67,"So we have to work a little more to get back to, oh, okay, that's that's how they did this, that's how they understood this, this was their custom at the time, you know, that kind of thing."],[1742.15,"Like, I mean, the big the big obvious one with with St."],[1744.95,"Paul's epistle to the Romans is we don't really have a church that's like a significant part Jewish converts, and then another part Gentiles, where there's this, you know, these issues back and forth."],[1761.93,"We don't have that."],[1762.65,"It's a different time."],[1764.09,"So, you know, that that's a big example, you know, where their situation is just sort of different."],[1770.05,"So the things that are super pertinent to them aren't as directly pertinent to us."],[1775.97,"So we have to figure out what the point St."],[1778.29,"Paul is making and then apply that to our own situation now."],[1783.65,"Yeah, so it's not identical, but yeah."],[1787.25,"Therefore, do not let sin reign in your mortal body, that you should obey it in its lusts."],[1795.73,"I'll pause there."],[1797.09,"So he talks about our mortal body."],[1801.31,"This is what we received from Adam."],[1805.15,"The story in Genesis 3 is not about how human beings became wicked and evil."],[1813.71,"I know you may have been told that, especially at Western churches."],[1814.27,"' Thats not what it's about."],[1820.31,"It's about why people die."],[1823.43,"That's what Genesis 3 is about."],[1827.83,"It's about why people die."],[1829.67,"People become more and more wicked after that."],[1833.59,"And it is the first time people sin, because that is how we become mortal."],[1840.47,"And the fathers teach this story in Genesis 3, where they're given clothing."],[1848.15,"That's how it's usually read by modern people."],[1851.75,"That God kills some animals and makes them clothes."],[1854.71,"Okay. That's not how the fathers understand that story."],[1858.87,"What it it says is, because doesn't say God made them clothes."],[1859.27,"says. It God kills some animals in front of them"],[1865.71,"And then he gives them skins."],[1871.09,"He gives them skins."],[1872.93,"So what that brief story is about is he kills the animals in front of them to show them what death is."],[1881.01,"Because they haven't seen it before."],[1887.81,"Kills the animals before them to show them what death is."],[1890.69,"And then their bodies change."],[1894.61,"I know that sounds strange."],[1897.49,"But let me put it to you on the reverse end."],[1900.41,"Okay, the reason the fathers say this is when Christ returns and we're raised from the dead, is our body gonna be identical to the one we have now?"],[1904.13,"Not for even"],[1910.49,"No. And when we get to 1 Corinthians 15, St."],[1913.91,"Paul talks about that at some length."],[1916.8,"How we have a mortal body now, it's gonna be clothed with immortality, it's gonna be different."],[1920.32,"It's gonna be like Christ's body after he rose from the dead."],[1923.76,"That's gonna change, okay."],[1925.2,"What the fathers are saying is, and how they're interpreting that passage in Genesis, is that the same thing happened sort of in reverse at the fall."],[1936.74,"That Adam and Eve's bodies before in paradise, in the presence of God, were different than they became afterwards."],[1947.46,"wards After they became mortal bodies."],[1947.62,"More like animal bodies"],[1950.56,"Bodies that are going to die."],[1954.32,"So are you talking about the shape of a body or the material that the bodies made out of?"],[1961.12,"St. Paul seems to indicate in 1 Corinthians 15 the material a body is made out of."],[1965.84,"Okay. But it's more in the likeness of an animal after the fall."],[1973.34,"Because animals die."],[1975.48,"That's that's our flesh."],[1978.16,"There's not a lot of there's not a lot of difference, you know, when you study biology, between our current human flesh and mammal, you know, that's and other mammals. yeah"],[1992.38,"Um but there was and there will be, is what scripture is saying."],[1998.79,"Because at the beginning we were created to be like God, so the image of God."],[2004.92,"So the image that we have, it looks like the image of God, but how we will be after the likeness, the likeness."],[2012.86,"Okay, that's the distinction the fathers make between the image and the likeness."],[2016.46,"It's confusing. Yeah, the the image is, and we'll talk about this more when we loop around and get back to Genesis."],[2022.35,"We'll we'll spend a lot of time going through this, but just a little bit of this is there to understand what what St."],[2028.11,"Paul is saying, and not just here, because St."],[2030.75,"Paul, and this is one of the ways that people misread St."],[2033.39,"Paul, is he'll talk about body, which is your physical body."],[2042.07,"And he'll also talk about the flesh."],[2045.99,"They're two different words."],[2048.23,"The word not Saint Paul."],[2051.59,"We use them the same way in English."],[2054.23,"But the word for flesh is more like meat."],[2058.47,"It really is."],[2060.07,"It really is in Hebrew, it's the same word."],[2061.99,"It's basar. It's the same word."],[2064.55,"Uh I guess the the Arabic word for meat is similar, isn't it?"],[2068.31,"No. Oh, for flesh?"],[2071.27,"Means the meat."],[2072.07,"Yeah, meat. What's the Arabic word for meat?"],[2074.87,"Lahm. Okay, it's different."],[2076.87,"Okay. Lahm. Yeah."],[2078.63,"So he uses those two words differently"],[2082.03,"If he's talking about the flesh, he's talking about our sinfulness."],[2086.51,"When he's talking about body, he's talking about our physicality."],[2089.87,"And those aren't the same thing."],[2092.51,"Because Christ has a physical body."],[2094.75,"But he's not sinful."],[2096.43,"So there's nothing wrong with having a body."],[2100.17,"But he talks that when he talks about the flesh, he's talking about the sinful part of us."],[2105.29,"Not our physical body."],[2107.15,"But the sinful part of us."],[2108.51,"Okay. And that's what he's doing here by putting mortal in front of body."],[2112.67,"He doesn't just mean our physical body."],[2116.67,"He's talking about the person who we are, which is still mortal."],[2121.87,"We're still gonna die."],[2122.67,"We're gonna have eternal life, and that eternal life starts now, but there's still gonna come a day when I stop breathing"],[2129.41,"And my soul departs from my body."],[2131.73,"And then that's then the resurrection's gonna happen."],[2135.01,"So I still have a mortal body."],[2137.73,"I still have, even though I have that person who I used to be is dead once I'm baptized."],[2145.17,"I don't instantly, when I come up out of the water, have a new body."],[2148.69,"That's like Jesus' resurrection body."],[2151.66,"That doesn't just happen all of a sudden."],[2153.34,"I still have this same flesh body that I inherited from Adam and Eve."],[2160.27,"I still have it."],[2161.39,"That means I can still sin if I choose to."],[2165.31,"Paul is addressing here"],[2165.67,"And that's what St."],[2168.63,"He's saying there's going to come a time in the kingdom when we're not going to be capable of sinning."],[2173.57,"We're not going to inadvertently sin."],[2175.09,"We're not going to accidentally sin in the kingdom after the resurrection."],[2179.73,"But now, even though we've died and risen with Christ in baptism, we can still sin."],[2187.81,"And so he has to say: don't let sin reign in your mortal body."],[2194.05,"Don't let sin come to control you again."],[2197.49,"Not just consider yourself dead to that, like you don't have to worry about it, but consider that dead like you're not going to go back to it."],[2204.71,"You're not going to let sin come to control you again."],[2209.43,"And you notice he says, sin rates your body so that you obey it"],[2213.21,"And its lusts and its passions."],[2217.61,"Where it becomes in charge."],[2220.09,"And do not present your members as instruments of unrighteousness as sin."],[2225.53,"But present yourselves to God as being alive from the dead, and your members as instruments of righteousness to God."],[2234.41,"So the word instruments there, probably a better modern translation would be tools."],[2240.97,"Because you use tools to do things."],[2243.45,"I take a hammer, I use it to drive a nail, take a wrench, I use it to screw or unscrew a bolt"],[2249.97,"You say you can offer yourself, your members, your parts."],[2256.05,"That's not just your physical parts, but that's your mind, your soul."],[2260.93,"You could offer them to be tools for sin, where sin is in control of you, and it's using your parts to accomplish evil things, to accomplish wickedness, cause harm, to cause violence."],[2276.61,"Or you could offer all of your parts to God to use as tools, to do righteousness, to do justice, to do good."],[2286.13,"He's saying you should do the latter and not the former"],[2290.17,"You should offer yourself to God so that God can do things through you rather than what used to happen, which was sin working evil in the world through you."],[2303.21,"So when he's talking about being freed from slavery, he doesn't mean now you're free to do whatever you want."],[2310.25,"To quote Bob Dylan again, you gotta serve somebody."],[2314.39,"So it's either going to be God or it's gonna be sin."],[2317.35,"One of these two things is gonna be acting through you in the world."],[2320.63,"And he's saying, now that that God, through Christ, has given you this new life and you're dead to that old way of living, now this is what it means to live that new life, and that's you offer yourself to God so that he can work his will and do his good in the world through you."],[2339.11,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God."],[2344.07,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaithcom."],[2351.79,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.16,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 6 - Recap of chapter 5","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at the beginning of <b>Romans 6</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Second half of <b>chapter 5</b> (from v.12) was dense - \"one of the most <b>controversial</b> passages in the Bible\"</li><li class=\"d1\">text from which <b>original sin</b> developed in the West</li><li class=\"d1\">major difference between <b>Eastern and Western Christianity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also a main text of the <b>Reformation</b> for Protestant doctrines</li><li class=\"d0\">Not going back through it - that Bible study will be on Ancient Faith (lead time close to a year)</li></ul>"},{"t":113.36,"lv":3,"h":"Rome background: Jews expelled by Claudius","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Under <b>Emperor Claudius</b> the Jewish community was expelled from Rome for a couple of years</li><li class=\"d1\">fragmented the Christian community: <b>Jewish Christians and Gentile Christians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Gentile Christians went on on their own; now Jewish Christians have <b>returned</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's concern: church <b>reintegrates</b>, not <b>two churches</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Intro Bible study on Romans also on Ancient Faith</li></ul>"},{"t":164.84,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's argument so far: Abraham to Adam","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">All human beings face the <b>same problems, same enemies</b>; Christ = the solution to the peril of humans as such</li><li class=\"d0\">Two things Jews saw separating them from Gentiles: <b>descent from Abraham</b> and <b>receiving the Torah</b> (law and customs)</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul first went <b>back before the law</b> to Abraham</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham lived <b>at least 500 years before Moses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how Abraham became <b>God's friend</b> - before the law, <b>before circumcision</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chapter 5</b>: back to <b>Adam</b> - includes everybody</li><li class=\"d1\">real problem isn't <b>tassels</b> or <b>pork</b></li><li class=\"d1\">everyone sins and therefore <b>everyone dies</b>, Jew or Gentile</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ = solution <b>regardless of ethnicity, culture</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ended last time: through Adam's sin <b>death reigned</b> until Christ; through his death and resurrection we receive <b>eternal life</b>, spreading with the gospel</li><li class=\"d0\">Chapter 5 = broad: Adam and Christ, <b>power of sin</b>; chapter 6 = <b>narrows to how it applies to you</b> in your actual life</li></ul>"},{"t":367.79,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 6:1-2 - Shall we continue in sin?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>6:1-2</b>: \"Shall we continue in sin that grace may abound? Certainly not. How shall we who died to sin live any longer in it?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Picks up from end of ch. 5: Torah added so <b>trespasses increase</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Adam: God gave direct commandment, he <b>deliberately sinned</b>, he died</li><li class=\"d1\">later people (Gentiles) with no direct command: <b>still sinned, still died</b>, just didn't know what sin was or what to do about it</li><li class=\"d1\">Torah → sins become <b>trespasses</b>; you know the rules and have <b>means of repentance</b> (sacrificial system) → reconciled to God</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Where sin abounded, grace abounded\" - more trespasses, but <b>repentance and forgiveness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Objection: so keep sinning, <b>the more we sin the more God forgives</b>?</li></ul>"},{"t":522.35,"lv":3,"h":"'Certainly not' - a super strong no","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Certainly not\" (<b>NKJV</b>) = odd Greek verb form, <b>optative</b></li><li class=\"d1\">only used <b>64 times</b> in the New Testament, <b>48 by Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">really old, antiquated form</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Super strong no\" = <b>\"heck no\"</b>; \"certainly not\" is the polite version</li><li class=\"d0\">\"That idea is obviously clueless\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Then: \"How shall we who <b>died to sin</b> live any longer in it?\" - he'll explain</li></ul>"},{"t":586.1,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 6:3-4 - Baptized into his death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>6:3</b>: as many as were <b>baptized into Christ Jesus</b> were baptized into his death</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Do you not know\" = his way of saying \"here's what I mean\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"As many of us\" = <b>everybody</b> baptized into Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">baptized into his death → when you went under the water, <b>you died with him</b></li></ul>"},{"t":594.62,"lv":3,"h":"'Christ Jesus' word order","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Word order reversed: <b>Christ Jesus</b> rather than Jesus Christ - \"don't read a ton into that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">English: subject, verb, object; Greek: <b>form of the word</b> tells part of speech</li><li class=\"d1\">so word order free - helps with <b>poetry and hymns</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reminder: <b>Christ is not Jesus' last name</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not Joseph and Mary's last name; once asked what Jesus' <b>middle name</b> was</li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>title</b>: <i>Christos</i> (Greek) = <i>Mashiach</i> (Hebrew) = <b>Messiah</b> = the anointed king</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Jesus the king\" or \"King Jesus\" - same thing</li></ul>"},{"t":686.79,"lv":3,"h":"Why we go all the way under","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Baptized into Christ = baptized into his death</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's why we put people <b>all the way under</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sprinkling doesn't represent <b>dying</b> - nobody ever died from water flicked in their face</li><li class=\"d1\">dogs and cats act like they will; no human has</li></ul>"},{"t":751.8,"lv":3,"h":"6:4 - Buried with him, newness of life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>6:4</b>: buried with him through baptism into death, that as Christ was raised by the <b>glory of the Father</b>, we should <b>walk in newness of life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Under the water you died; coming up = <b>beginning of a new life</b>, as Christ died and rose</li><li class=\"d0\">Everything that characterized you <b>before baptism</b> is now dead</li><li class=\"d0\">Referring back to <b>Adam</b>: sin entered through one man, death to everyone because all sinned</li><li class=\"d1\">\"like father, like son and daughter\": sinned like him, died like him</li><li class=\"d1\">born with the <b>fallen human nature</b> inherited from him - <b>born mortal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not born immortal and then sin makes us able to die</li><li class=\"d0\">The person you were, with all you inherited, <b>died in the water</b></li><li class=\"d1\">person who came out = new, \"after the pattern of Christ in his resurrection\"</li></ul>"},{"t":863.58,"lv":3,"h":"Eternal life starts now","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Related to <b>eternal life</b> (as in <b>John's Gospel</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">We think eternal life comes <b>after</b>: die, soul goes somewhere, resurrection when Christ returns, then it starts</li><li class=\"d0\">Not what Paul says: <b>coming up out of the water, your eternal life started</b></li><li class=\"d1\">begins now, continues into eternity</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's whole point: <b>we can't go on sinning</b>, can't live as we used to</li><li class=\"d1\">in the kingdom after the last-day resurrection, are you sinning? \"<b>No</b>\" - not a trick question</li><li class=\"d1\">but the <b>kingdom has started now</b>; you're a member now</li><li class=\"d1\">so how could you go on living the way you used to? \"<b>Not possible</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Main reason for the argument: criticism from <b>some in the Jewish community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"he's teaching against the law\", \"get rid of it\", \"don't have to keep the commandments\", \"live however\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: \"no, quite the opposite\" - <b>impossible</b> to keep living the old way</li></ul>"},{"t":1007.64,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 6:5 - Already and not yet","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>6:5</b>: united in the likeness of his death → certainly also in the likeness of his <b>resurrection</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul uses resurrection and new life <b>both ways</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not denying the <b>resurrection on the last day</b> - it begins now</li><li class=\"d0\">For Paul, <b>already and not yet</b> for all these things</li><li class=\"d1\">already: Christ's promises, <b>new life</b>, eternal life started, a different type of life, the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not yet: those same things <b>in their fullness</b> - literally raised from the dead when Christ returns</li><li class=\"d0\">Not two separate things: the <b>kingdom has already begun and is going to come</b> - both true at the same time</li></ul>"},{"t":1112.54,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 6:6-7 - Old man crucified","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>6:6-7</b>: <b>old man</b> crucified with him so the body of sin might be done away with, no longer <b>slaves of sin</b>; he who has died is <b>freed from sin</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The person who was a slave of sin is <b>dead</b> - if you were baptized</li><li class=\"d0\">Dead people can't sin: \"they're just laying there... they don't have bodies\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Brings in the idea of <b>slavery</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1156.08,"lv":3,"h":"Sin vs sins - sin as a power","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">For Paul a distinction between <b>sin</b> and <b>sins</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sins</b> = individual <b>trespasses</b>: God gave a commandment, you broke it; lots to confess and repent of</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sin</b> = general, a <b>power</b> - Romans 5: death reigns through sin; death = overlord</li><li class=\"d0\">Goes back to <b>Genesis 4</b>: Cain angry at Abel</li><li class=\"d1\">God: \"<b>sin is crouching at your door</b>\" - like an animal ready to pounce</li><li class=\"d1\">\"seeks to master you, you must master it\" - battle between you and sin; <b>Cain falls prey</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Slaves of sin\" = sin comes in, <b>masters us</b>, takes control</li><li class=\"d1\">we think of sins as voluntary actions; this is sin as <b>force or power</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why Orthodox talk about the <b>passions</b>: passion from the same root as <b>passive</b></li><li class=\"d1\">acts upon us; we become passive and it takes control</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pride</b> controls our actions; <b>envy</b>, <b>anger</b> (\"we lost control\", then regret), <b>sloth</b>, <b>lust</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"once in a while we did some bad things\" - <b>sin had taken control</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1341.04,"lv":3,"h":"Why the slave must die","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not enough to offer the <b>87th goat</b> for this week's sins</li><li class=\"d1\">that's <b>managing</b> the problem, not dealing with it</li><li class=\"d1\">the Torah <b>manages sin</b>, keeps it under control</li><li class=\"d0\">To deal with it: <b>we as slaves have to die</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a slave who dies is free - \"the only way out\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Through baptism, Christ's death and resurrection, <b>we die with him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">slavery over; rise to new life <b>free from sin</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Individual sins = <b>result</b> of being controlled by sin</li><li class=\"d1\">no longer slave → <b>no reason</b> to commit those actions</li><li class=\"d1\">now alive and free, we can <b>live a different way</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1435.21,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 6:8-10 - Christ dies no more","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>6:8-10</b>: if we died with Christ we shall also live with him; Christ raised <b>dies no more</b>, death no longer has dominion</li><li class=\"d0\">Difference from others raised in the Bible</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Lazarus</b> lived a few more years and died again</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>widow of Nain's son</b> likewise</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>never</b> dies again</li><li class=\"d0\">For us: died with Christ, raised to new life → <b>death has no power over us either</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what we celebrate at <b>Pascha</b> (50 days from now - \"Christ trampling down death by death\")</li><li class=\"d0\">\"He died to sin once for all; the life he lives he lives to God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he didn't die to <b>his</b> sins - had none</li><li class=\"d1\">for us: the sinful person we used to be, the sins committed = dead</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ ascended to the Father - lives <b>to God</b> very literally; we can live <b>toward God, in his presence</b>, because set free</li></ul>"},{"t":1550.49,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 6:11 - Reckon yourselves dead","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>6:11</b>: reckon yourselves <b>dead indeed to sin, but alive to God</b> in Christ Jesus our Lord</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Reckon\" = \"in your head, <b>wrap your head around this</b>\": if baptized, this is what happened to you</li><li class=\"d1\">slavery to sin, the sinful person, the old person = <b>dead</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Leave them behind. Start new. Start again.\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1581.82,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Did the Romans understand Paul?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: did people really understand what Paul meant - very mixed together?</li><li class=\"d0\">Most of his letters to churches where he'd been, many he started - they'd <b>heard him preach</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Rome</b> he hasn't visited yet; but they'd received preaching from others</li><li class=\"d0\">Baptism: apostles had a <b>way they baptized</b> - a <b>liturgical</b> thing</li><li class=\"d1\">they'd been baptized, seen baptisms, heard the <b>hymns</b> and preaching</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul refers back to it and explains what it means</li><li class=\"d0\">We're not in that culture → <b>why we need Bible studies</b></li><li class=\"d1\">analogy: <b>Metropolitan Joseph's</b> letter - we understand; outsiders (even in England, non-Orthodox) would puzzle over phrases, references to hymns</li><li class=\"d1\">\"same kind of thing, just 2,000 years later\" - work to see how they understood and their customs</li><li class=\"d0\">Big difference: no church today that's <b>part Jewish converts, part Gentiles</b> with those issues</li><li class=\"d1\">what's pertinent to them isn't directly to us → find Paul's <b>point</b> and apply it to our situation</li></ul>"},{"t":1805.15,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 6:12 - Mortal body and Genesis 3","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>6:12</b>: do not let sin <b>reign in your mortal body</b>, that you should obey it in its lusts</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Mortal body\" = what we received from <b>Adam</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Genesis 3</b> is not about how humans became wicked - despite what Western churches may teach</li><li class=\"d1\">it's about <b>why people die</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people become more wicked after; first sin = how we become <b>mortal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers on the <b>clothing</b> story</li><li class=\"d1\">moderns read: God kills animals and <b>makes them clothes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">text: God kills animals <b>in front of them</b>, then gives them <b>skins</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fathers: shows them <b>what death is</b> - they hadn't seen it; <b>then their bodies change</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reverse side: at Christ's return, our bodies won't be identical</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 Corinthians 15</b>: mortal body clothed with <b>immortality</b>, like Christ's risen body</li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers: same in reverse at the fall</li><li class=\"d1\">bodies in paradise, in God's presence, <b>different</b> from after</li><li class=\"d1\">became mortal bodies, <b>more like animal bodies</b>, bodies that die</li></ul>"},{"t":1954.32,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Shape or material? Image and likeness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: shape of a body or material it's made of?</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul in <b>1 Corinthians 15</b> seems to indicate the <b>material</b></li><li class=\"d0\">After the fall more in the <b>likeness of an animal</b> - animals die</li><li class=\"d1\">little difference between human flesh and other <b>mammals</b> in biology</li><li class=\"d0\">Scripture: there was and there will be a difference</li><li class=\"d1\">created to be like God: <b>image</b> of God</li><li class=\"d1\">how we'll be after the <b>likeness</b> - the fathers' distinction between <b>image and likeness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Will return to it when looping back to <b>Genesis</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2030.75,"lv":3,"h":"Body vs flesh in Paul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Misreading of Paul: he talks about <b>body</b> and also about the <b>flesh</b> - two different words</li><li class=\"d1\">English uses them alike; flesh is more like <b>meat</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew <i>basar</i> = same word; Q: Arabic? <i>lahm</i> - different</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Flesh</b> = our <b>sinfulness</b>; <b>body</b> = our <b>physicality</b> - not the same</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ has a physical body but isn't sinful → nothing wrong with having a body</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mortal</b> in front of body (6:12) = not just the physical body</li><li class=\"d1\">the person we are, <b>still mortal</b>, still going to die</li></ul>"},{"t":2112.67,"lv":3,"h":"Still mortal, still able to sin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Eternal life starts now, but a day will come when <b>I stop breathing</b>, my soul departs, then the resurrection</li><li class=\"d0\">Baptized: old person dead, but I don't instantly get a <b>resurrection body</b></li><li class=\"d1\">still the same flesh body inherited from <b>Adam and Eve</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ I can still <b>sin if I choose to</b></li><li class=\"d0\">In the kingdom: not capable of sinning, not inadvertently or accidentally</li><li class=\"d0\">Now, even though died and risen in baptism, <b>we can still sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so Paul: <b>don't let sin reign</b> in your mortal body, don't let it control you again</li><li class=\"d1\">not just \"consider yourself dead to that, don't worry\" but dead as in <b>not going back</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Obey it in its <b>lusts and passions</b>\" - where it's <b>in charge</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2220.09,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 6:13 - Tools of righteousness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>6:13</b>: don't present your members as instruments of <b>unrighteousness</b> to sin; present yourselves to God as <b>alive from the dead</b>, members as instruments of <b>righteousness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Instruments\" better as <b>tools</b> - hammer drives a nail, wrench turns a bolt</li><li class=\"d0\">Your members = physical parts, <b>mind, soul</b> too</li><li class=\"d1\">tools for <b>sin</b>: in control, using you for wickedness, harm, <b>violence</b></li><li class=\"d1\">or tools for <b>God</b>: righteousness, justice, good</li><li class=\"d0\">Offer yourself so God can do things through you, rather than <b>sin working evil through you</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2303.21,"lv":3,"h":"Free from slavery is not free to do anything","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Freed from slavery ≠ free to do whatever you want</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Bob Dylan</b> again: \"you gotta <b>serve somebody</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">either <b>God</b> or <b>sin</b> acts through you in the world</li><li class=\"d0\">God through Christ gave you new life; dead to the old way</li><li class=\"d1\">living it = <b>offer yourself to God</b> so he works his will and does his good in the world through you</li></ul>"}]});
